# A repeat with no count goes on for ever: the lamp's lights come round in the same order, so any
# light far along can be worked out from the part that repeats.
lesson coding-a-pattern-that-never-ends v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=2 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A pattern that never ends"
  goal "Say what a lamp that repeats for ever shows at any step, from the part of its program that repeats."

  look {
    scene 28x17 {
      row all space=2 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        lighthouse tower stripes=3 beam=1
        column side space=1 align=start {
          program p code=["repeat for ever", "  light red", "  light green"] title="Pip's lamp"
          pixels lights cols=6 rows=1 code=["repeat for ever", "  light red", "  light green"] show=true beside=false
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      At the lamp rocks Pip's lamp shows red, then green, then red, then green, all night long. Its
      program says repeat for ever: after the last line it goes back to the top, again and again,
      and never stops. A repeat with a number, like repeat 3, stops after going round three times.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice coding.forever-next-light count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    level easy {
      worked coding.forever-light-at-step u=0 n=4 level=medium
    }
    practice coding.forever-light-at-step count=2 seed=10 easy-seed=8 hard-seed=1
    practice coding.forever-how-many count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice coding.forever-which-program count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=5
    level hard {
      show coding.forever-all-night s=0 r=1 c=1
    }
    practice coding.forever-lamp-night count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.forever-all-night s=0 r=0 c=0 easy-r=1 hard-s=1 hard-c=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.forever-fewest-lines k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  remember {
    say "Repeat for ever goes back to the top every time and never stops."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a repeat with no number never stops, and that a pattern made by one can be
    worked out far ahead from the part that repeats. The wrong ideas to watch for are thinking a big
    number is the same as for ever, and counting along the lights one by one without using the
    repeat. In the working, look for the child marking where the pattern starts again. The two-star
    question compares a repeat with a number and a repeat for ever; the three-star question finds
    the shortest part that repeats, which is the fewest lines the program needs. A child who is
    stuck can say the colours aloud with a finger on each light, and go back to the way in; stay on
    this lesson another day if far lights are still counted one by one. Off the page, flash a torch
    in a pattern and let the child say what the tenth flash will be.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the next light follows a pattern of two colours, light 4 is worked once in the teacher's pen before lights 2 and 3 of other patterns, the programs to count repeat twice or for ever, the red lights are counted over 4 minutes, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star's lights repeat every two."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the next light follows a pattern with a colour twice in the part that repeats, the lights asked are numbers 7 and 9, the programs to count repeat three and four times, a second pair of programs is judged before the two-star, the red lights are counted over 7 minutes, where the odd minute adds one, and the three-star's lights repeat every four."
  }
}
